1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,128
For nearly thirty years, the state of

2
00:00:02,128 --> 00:00:04,371
Alabama kept a date to kill Anthony Ray

3
00:00:04,371 --> 00:00:05,071
Hinton —

4
00:00:05,071 --> 00:00:07,026
for two murders he did not commit.

5
00:00:07,026 --> 00:00:09,441
The entire case rested on a single bullet.

6
00:00:09,441 --> 00:00:11,224
And that bullet, it turned out,

7
00:00:11,224 --> 00:00:12,307
matched nothing at all.

8
00:00:12,657 --> 00:00:14,740
This is the story of how one piece

9
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:17,190
of "science" and a broke defense sent an

10
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:19,762
innocent man to death row for nearly three

11
00:00:19,762 --> 00:00:20,462
decades —

12
00:00:20,462 --> 00:00:22,974
how the system almost killed him for it —

13
00:00:22,974 --> 00:00:25,179
and how it never managed to take the

14
00:00:25,179 --> 00:00:26,868
one thing that kept him alive.

15
00:00:27,218 --> 00:00:29,556
In 1985, around Birmingham, Alabama,

16
00:00:29,556 --> 00:00:32,089
two fast-food managers were murdered in

17
00:00:32,089 --> 00:00:34,102
separate late-night robberies —

18
00:00:34,102 --> 00:00:36,635
John Davidson in February, Thomas Wayne

19
00:00:36,635 --> 00:00:37,544
Vason in July.

20
00:00:37,544 --> 00:00:39,752
Both were killed for the cash in a

21
00:00:39,752 --> 00:00:40,791
restaurant safe.

22
00:00:40,791 --> 00:00:43,714
There were no eyewitnesses
to either killing.

23
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:45,467
There were no fingerprints.

24
00:00:45,467 --> 00:00:47,805
For months, the cases went nowhere —

25
00:00:47,805 --> 00:00:50,338
and an unsolved murder of a working man

26
00:00:50,338 --> 00:00:52,676
is exactly the kind of case a police

27
00:00:52,676 --> 00:00:54,754
department badly needs to close.

28
00:00:54,754 --> 00:00:57,222
Pressure to solve a killing is its own

29
00:00:57,222 --> 00:00:59,885
kind of force; it bends everything toward

30
00:00:59,885 --> 00:01:02,288
an answer, any answer that will hold.

31
00:01:02,288 --> 00:01:03,457
Two men were dead.

32
00:01:03,457 --> 00:01:05,795
A city wanted someone to pay for it.

33
00:01:05,795 --> 00:01:08,393
And when the survivor of a third robbery

34
00:01:08,393 --> 00:01:10,666
pointed to a face in a photo array,

35
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:13,264
the machinery of a capital case began to

36
00:01:13,264 --> 00:01:13,964
move —

37
00:01:13,964 --> 00:01:16,367
and it would not pause to ask whether

38
00:01:16,367 --> 00:01:17,420
it had the right man.

39
00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:20,751
Then, weeks later, a third
restaurant was robbed.

40
00:01:20,751 --> 00:01:23,246
This time the manager, a man named Sidney

41
00:01:23,246 --> 00:01:25,436
Smotherman, survived being shot at —

42
00:01:25,436 --> 00:01:27,383
and later picked a face out of a

43
00:01:27,383 --> 00:01:28,174
photo lineup.

44
00:01:28,174 --> 00:01:29,512
The face belonged to a

45
00:01:29,512 --> 00:01:31,703
twenty-nine-year-old Black man named

46
00:01:31,703 --> 00:01:32,859
Anthony Ray Hinton.

47
00:01:32,859 --> 00:01:35,171
That identification is the only reason

48
00:01:35,171 --> 00:01:37,179
Hinton entered this story at all.

49
00:01:37,179 --> 00:01:39,551
There was nothing else tying him to the

50
00:01:39,551 --> 00:01:40,707
first two murders —

51
00:01:40,707 --> 00:01:42,898
no witness, no print, no confession.

52
00:01:42,898 --> 00:01:45,392
Nothing except what the police believed a

53
00:01:45,392 --> 00:01:46,609
gun could tell them.

54
00:01:46,959 --> 00:01:48,981
But here is the fact the jury would

55
00:01:48,981 --> 00:01:50,252
be asked to set aside.

56
00:01:50,252 --> 00:01:52,332
At the moment of that third robbery,

57
00:01:52,332 --> 00:01:54,758
Anthony Ray Hinton was fifteen miles away,

58
00:01:54,758 --> 00:01:56,953
locked inside a supermarket warehouse,

59
00:01:56,953 --> 00:01:58,224
working a night shift.

60
00:01:58,224 --> 00:01:59,264
He was clocked in.

61
00:01:59,264 --> 00:02:01,286
A supervisor could place him there.

62
00:02:01,286 --> 00:02:03,597
His own time card could place him there.

63
00:02:03,597 --> 00:02:05,677
He could not have been in two places

64
00:02:05,677 --> 00:02:06,377
at once.

65
00:02:06,489 --> 00:02:08,144
In almost any other case,

66
00:02:08,144 --> 00:02:10,330
that is where a prosecution ends.

67
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:12,449
A man locked inside a warehouse,

68
00:02:12,449 --> 00:02:14,104
on the clock, miles away,

69
00:02:14,104 --> 00:02:15,428
is not your suspect.

70
00:02:15,428 --> 00:02:18,077
But the state had already decided who it

71
00:02:18,077 --> 00:02:19,203
was looking for —

72
00:02:19,203 --> 00:02:21,719
and it believed it had found something

73
00:02:21,719 --> 00:02:23,904
that could beat any alibi at all.

74
00:02:24,254 --> 00:02:26,112
That something was the bullets.

75
00:02:26,462 --> 00:02:29,005
Prosecutors claimed the bullets from the

76
00:02:29,005 --> 00:02:31,484
crime scenes had been fired from an old

77
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:33,836
.38-caliber revolver that belonged to

78
00:02:33,836 --> 00:02:36,443
Hinton's mother, recovered from her home.

79
00:02:36,443 --> 00:02:38,159
There were no fingerprints.

80
00:02:38,159 --> 00:02:40,321
No eyewitness who saw the murders.

81
00:02:40,321 --> 00:02:41,211
No confession.

82
00:02:41,211 --> 00:02:43,118
There were only those bullets,

83
00:02:43,118 --> 00:02:44,580
and a gun that had sat,

84
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,932
for years, in an aging woman's house.

85
00:02:46,932 --> 00:02:49,285
If the bullets matched that revolver,

86
00:02:49,285 --> 00:02:50,556
the case was closed.

87
00:02:50,556 --> 00:02:52,972
If they didn't, the state had nothing.

88
00:02:52,972 --> 00:02:55,642
The entire question of whether a man would

89
00:02:55,642 --> 00:02:57,613
live or die came down to a duel

90
00:02:57,613 --> 00:02:59,202
between firearms experts.

91
00:02:59,552 --> 00:03:02,450
And this is where being
poor decides everything.

92
00:03:02,450 --> 00:03:04,745
To fight the state's ballistics claim,

93
00:03:04,745 --> 00:03:07,100
Hinton needed a real forensic expert of

94
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:07,800
his own.

95
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:10,335
But his court-appointed lawyer was working

96
00:03:10,335 --> 00:03:12,509
under a belief that turned out to be

97
00:03:12,509 --> 00:03:14,924
false: he thought Alabama law capped the

98
00:03:14,924 --> 00:03:17,219
money for a defense expert at just one

99
00:03:17,219 --> 00:03:18,245
thousand dollars.

100
00:03:18,245 --> 00:03:19,574
There was no such cap.

101
00:03:19,574 --> 00:03:21,928
The law would have paid for a qualified

102
00:03:21,928 --> 00:03:24,102
expert, if he had only asked for it.

103
00:03:24,102 --> 00:03:24,947
He didn't ask.

104
00:03:24,947 --> 00:03:26,698
He took the thousand dollars,

105
00:03:26,698 --> 00:03:28,872
and with it he hired the only person

106
00:03:28,872 --> 00:03:30,527
willing to work for that price.

107
00:03:30,877 --> 00:03:33,163
The man he found was a civil engineer

108
00:03:33,163 --> 00:03:34,337
named Andrew Payne.

109
00:03:34,337 --> 00:03:36,685
On the stand, under cross-examination,

110
00:03:36,685 --> 00:03:39,157
Payne admitted that he had sight in only

111
00:03:39,157 --> 00:03:39,857
one eye —

112
00:03:39,857 --> 00:03:42,452
and that he could not properly operate the

113
00:03:42,452 --> 00:03:45,047
comparison microscope, the very instrument

114
00:03:45,047 --> 00:03:47,024
used to match a bullet to a gun.

115
00:03:47,024 --> 00:03:49,557
The prosecutor took him apart in front of

116
00:03:49,557 --> 00:03:51,102
the jury, piece by piece.

117
00:03:51,102 --> 00:03:53,574
The one witness standing between Anthony

118
00:03:53,574 --> 00:03:55,983
Ray Hinton and the electric chair could

119
00:03:55,983 --> 00:03:58,826
not see, and could not
work his own equipment.

120
00:03:58,826 --> 00:04:01,112
Picture it: the man hired to defend a

121
00:04:01,112 --> 00:04:03,707
capital case, squinting into an instrument

122
00:04:03,707 --> 00:04:06,302
he could not operate, while the prosecutor

123
00:04:06,302 --> 00:04:08,835
asked him again and again to explain what

124
00:04:08,835 --> 00:04:09,535
he saw.

125
00:04:09,535 --> 00:04:10,338
He could not.

126
00:04:10,338 --> 00:04:12,686
And a jury watched the only science on

127
00:04:12,686 --> 00:04:14,623
Hinton's side come apart in real time.

128
00:04:14,973 --> 00:04:17,186
Understand what that thousand-dollar

129
00:04:17,186 --> 00:04:18,354
mistake really was.

130
00:04:18,354 --> 00:04:20,752
In a case built entirely on the science

131
00:04:20,752 --> 00:04:22,596
of matching a bullet to a gun,

132
00:04:22,596 --> 00:04:24,625
a man's life depended on having a

133
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:26,776
scientist who could actually do it.

134
00:04:26,776 --> 00:04:28,928
The money was there for the asking.

135
00:04:28,928 --> 00:04:31,387
His lawyer, wrongly believing it wasn't,

136
00:04:31,387 --> 00:04:32,125
never asked.

137
00:04:32,125 --> 00:04:34,645
That single unasked question is what sent

138
00:04:34,645 --> 00:04:36,428
an innocent man toward death.

139
00:04:36,778 --> 00:04:38,470
The jury took about an hour.

140
00:04:38,470 --> 00:04:40,886
In September of 1986, they convicted him

141
00:04:40,886 --> 00:04:43,605
of both murders, and he
was sentenced to die.

142
00:04:43,955 --> 00:04:46,373
Hinton always said what he believed put

143
00:04:46,373 --> 00:04:47,073
him there.

144
00:04:47,073 --> 00:04:47,879
He was Black.

145
00:04:47,879 --> 00:04:48,623
He was poor.

146
00:04:48,623 --> 00:04:51,103
He could not afford a defense that could

147
00:04:51,103 --> 00:04:51,803
fight back.

148
00:04:51,803 --> 00:04:53,291
And in Alabama, in 1986,

149
00:04:53,291 --> 00:04:54,283
that was enough.

150
00:04:54,283 --> 00:04:56,577
He would say later that he understood

151
00:04:56,577 --> 00:04:59,058
exactly what he must have looked like to

152
00:04:59,058 --> 00:05:01,600
that jury: a poor Black man from Alabama,

153
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,460
accused of killing two people,

154
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:05,940
with a broken-down witness for a defense

155
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:08,420
and a court-appointed lawyer who had not

156
00:05:08,420 --> 00:05:10,590
fought for the money to defend him.

157
00:05:10,590 --> 00:05:11,954
In a system like that,

158
00:05:11,954 --> 00:05:14,124
he came to believe, the verdict was

159
00:05:14,124 --> 00:05:16,356
written before the trial ever began.

160
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:18,402
And it is hard to argue with him.

161
00:05:18,402 --> 00:05:19,766
The evidence was junk.

162
00:05:19,766 --> 00:05:21,131
The defense was broke.

163
00:05:21,131 --> 00:05:23,301
The jury was out for about an hour.

164
00:05:23,301 --> 00:05:24,913
In that room, on that day,

165
00:05:24,913 --> 00:05:27,145
the outcome had less to do with what

166
00:05:27,145 --> 00:05:30,023
had happened than with
who was standing accused.

167
00:05:30,373 --> 00:05:32,044
He was sent to a cell the size

168
00:05:32,044 --> 00:05:34,105
of a bathroom on Alabama's death row.

169
00:05:34,105 --> 00:05:36,222
And he would stay there for the better

170
00:05:36,222 --> 00:05:37,392
part of thirty years.

171
00:05:37,392 --> 00:05:38,840
For the first three years,

172
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:39,787
he barely spoke —

173
00:05:39,787 --> 00:05:41,959
swallowed by rage and grief at a system

174
00:05:41,959 --> 00:05:43,185
that would not listen.

175
00:05:43,185 --> 00:05:45,190
And then Anthony Ray Hinton made the

176
00:05:45,190 --> 00:05:47,474
decision that is the heart of this story.

177
00:05:47,474 --> 00:05:49,535
He decided that a cell could hold his

178
00:05:49,535 --> 00:05:51,596
body, but it would not hold his mind.

179
00:05:51,946 --> 00:05:53,493
He started a book club —

180
00:05:53,493 --> 00:05:56,136
condemned men meeting among the law books

181
00:05:56,136 --> 00:05:58,843
to argue about writers like James Baldwin.

182
00:05:58,843 --> 00:06:01,099
In his imagination, he simply left.

183
00:06:01,099 --> 00:06:02,517
He traveled the world.

184
00:06:02,517 --> 00:06:03,999
He married movie stars.

185
00:06:03,999 --> 00:06:07,093
He played center field
for the New York Yankees.

186
00:06:07,093 --> 00:06:09,220
He found humor in a place with no

187
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:10,187
reason for any.

188
00:06:10,187 --> 00:06:12,056
You can lock up a man's body,

189
00:06:12,056 --> 00:06:12,959
he understood.

190
00:06:12,959 --> 00:06:15,601
You cannot lock up what he allows himself

191
00:06:15,601 --> 00:06:16,310
to imagine.

192
00:06:16,310 --> 00:06:18,695
There were seven of them in that book

193
00:06:18,695 --> 00:06:19,920
club at the start —

194
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:22,563
condemned men passing paperbacks down the

195
00:06:22,563 --> 00:06:25,076
row, arguing about the ending as if any

196
00:06:25,076 --> 00:06:27,526
of them had all the time in the world.

197
00:06:27,526 --> 00:06:28,364
They did not.

198
00:06:28,364 --> 00:06:30,297
But for an hour, in a house of

199
00:06:30,297 --> 00:06:33,004
the sentenced, a book could make the walls

200
00:06:33,004 --> 00:06:33,704
disappear.

201
00:06:33,999 --> 00:06:36,471
From the outside, that can sound like a

202
00:06:36,471 --> 00:06:37,295
small thing —

203
00:06:37,295 --> 00:06:40,274
a man telling himself
stories to pass the time.

204
00:06:40,274 --> 00:06:41,351
It was not small.

205
00:06:41,351 --> 00:06:43,823
It was the difference between surviving

206
00:06:43,823 --> 00:06:44,900
and being erased.

207
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:46,929
The state had taken his freedom,

208
00:06:46,929 --> 00:06:49,591
his good name, nearly three decades of his

209
00:06:49,591 --> 00:06:50,291
one life.

210
00:06:50,291 --> 00:06:52,762
The single thing it could not reach was

211
00:06:52,762 --> 00:06:55,424
the country he built inside his own head —

212
00:06:55,424 --> 00:06:57,769
and he defended it like the last free

213
00:06:57,769 --> 00:06:58,844
ground he had left.

214
00:06:59,194 --> 00:07:01,309
But imagination is not innocence,

215
00:07:01,309 --> 00:07:02,974
and the years kept coming.

216
00:07:02,974 --> 00:07:05,281
He had founded that book club with a

217
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:07,332
handful of other condemned men —

218
00:07:07,332 --> 00:07:09,318
and one by one, over the years,

219
00:07:09,318 --> 00:07:11,753
he watched its members walked past his

220
00:07:11,753 --> 00:07:14,124
cell to be executed, until he was the

221
00:07:14,124 --> 00:07:15,982
last of them still breathing.

222
00:07:15,982 --> 00:07:17,776
Over his decades on the row,

223
00:07:17,776 --> 00:07:20,147
Hinton says he counted fifty-four men

224
00:07:20,147 --> 00:07:21,941
taken to the electric chair,

225
00:07:21,941 --> 00:07:23,863
close enough to know each one.

226
00:07:23,863 --> 00:07:25,850
Any year, it could be his door.

227
00:07:25,850 --> 00:07:27,964
He came to know the sound of it —

228
00:07:27,964 --> 00:07:30,335
the particular quiet of the row on an

229
00:07:30,335 --> 00:07:32,962
execution night, the footsteps, the smell

230
00:07:32,962 --> 00:07:33,859
that followed.

231
00:07:33,859 --> 00:07:35,846
One by one, the men of his book

232
00:07:35,846 --> 00:07:38,473
club were taken, until Anthony Ray Hinton

233
00:07:38,473 --> 00:07:40,715
was the only one of them left alive

234
00:07:40,715 --> 00:07:42,894
to remember they had ever existed.

235
00:07:43,244 --> 00:07:45,475
Then a lawyer named Bryan Stevenson,

236
00:07:45,475 --> 00:07:47,520
and his Equal Justice Initiative,

237
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:48,760
took Hinton's case —

238
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:51,611
and would fight it for
the next sixteen years.

239
00:07:51,611 --> 00:07:54,214
Sixteen years spent trying to make a state

240
00:07:54,214 --> 00:07:56,507
admit it had condemned the wrong man.

241
00:07:56,507 --> 00:07:58,738
In 2002, Stevenson did the one thing

242
00:07:58,738 --> 00:08:00,907
Hinton's first defense never could.

243
00:08:00,907 --> 00:08:03,014
He put three of the most qualified

244
00:08:03,014 --> 00:08:05,370
firearms experts in the country on the

245
00:08:05,370 --> 00:08:06,070
stand —

246
00:08:06,070 --> 00:08:07,681
one of them from the FBI —

247
00:08:07,681 --> 00:08:09,974
and they testified, plainly, that the

248
00:08:09,974 --> 00:08:12,577
crime bullets could not be matched to that

249
00:08:12,577 --> 00:08:13,277
revolver.

250
00:08:13,277 --> 00:08:15,880
They could not even be certain the bullets

251
00:08:15,880 --> 00:08:17,145
had come from a single gun.

252
00:08:17,495 --> 00:08:20,305
This was not a loophole
or a clever defense.

253
00:08:20,305 --> 00:08:22,733
It was the plain truth of the physical

254
00:08:22,733 --> 00:08:25,288
evidence, told by the best in the field.

255
00:08:25,288 --> 00:08:27,843
The one thread the entire death sentence

256
00:08:27,843 --> 00:08:30,014
hung from had never actually held.

257
00:08:30,014 --> 00:08:30,717
And still —

258
00:08:30,717 --> 00:08:32,952
for more than a decade after that —

259
00:08:32,952 --> 00:08:35,699
the state of Alabama
refused to let him go.

260
00:08:36,049 --> 00:08:38,362
In the end, it took the Supreme Court

261
00:08:38,362 --> 00:08:39,675
of the United States.

262
00:08:39,675 --> 00:08:40,988
In 2014, in Hinton v.

263
00:08:40,988 --> 00:08:42,613
Alabama, the Court ruled —

264
00:08:42,613 --> 00:08:44,989
and it was unanimous, nine to nothing.

265
00:08:44,989 --> 00:08:47,115
Every justice agreed that Hinton's

266
00:08:47,115 --> 00:08:49,678
original trial had been unconstitutional,

267
00:08:49,678 --> 00:08:52,179
because his lawyer's failure to seek the

268
00:08:52,179 --> 00:08:53,742
money for a real expert —

269
00:08:53,742 --> 00:08:56,367
a failure built on that false belief about

270
00:08:56,367 --> 00:08:57,805
a thousand-dollar cap —

271
00:08:57,805 --> 00:08:59,681
had denied him a real defense.

272
00:08:59,681 --> 00:09:01,056
The right to a lawyer,

273
00:09:01,056 --> 00:09:02,682
the Court has always said,

274
00:09:02,682 --> 00:09:04,932
means the right to a lawyer who does

275
00:09:04,932 --> 00:09:05,870
the basic work.

276
00:09:05,870 --> 00:09:07,871
In a case that lived and died on

277
00:09:07,871 --> 00:09:10,434
expert testimony, asking for the money to

278
00:09:10,434 --> 00:09:12,872
hire a competent expert was as basic as

279
00:09:12,872 --> 00:09:13,572
it gets.

280
00:09:13,572 --> 00:09:15,260
The verdict was thrown out.

281
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:16,498
The case was sent back.

282
00:09:16,848 --> 00:09:19,259
And when the state finally had to prove

283
00:09:19,259 --> 00:09:21,669
its ballistics match again, with modern

284
00:09:21,669 --> 00:09:22,967
experts, it couldn't.

285
00:09:22,967 --> 00:09:25,378
The evidence that had sent him to death

286
00:09:25,378 --> 00:09:27,603
row simply could not be reproduced —

287
00:09:27,603 --> 00:09:29,519
because it had never been true.

288
00:09:29,519 --> 00:09:31,930
With no case left, prosecutors moved to

289
00:09:31,930 --> 00:09:32,981
drop the charges.

290
00:09:32,981 --> 00:09:35,144
It had taken sixteen years of Bryan

291
00:09:35,144 --> 00:09:37,493
Stevenson's fight, and thirty years of

292
00:09:37,493 --> 00:09:39,718
Anthony Ray Hinton's life, to undo a

293
00:09:39,718 --> 00:09:42,129
one-hour verdict built on a bullet that

294
00:09:42,129 --> 00:09:42,994
never matched.

295
00:09:43,344 --> 00:09:45,701
On April 3rd, 2015, Anthony Ray Hinton

296
00:09:45,701 --> 00:09:47,687
walked out of prison a free man,

297
00:09:47,687 --> 00:09:49,362
after nearly thirty years —

298
00:09:49,362 --> 00:09:51,905
the hundred-and-fifty-second person freed

299
00:09:51,905 --> 00:09:54,201
from death row in America after being

300
00:09:54,201 --> 00:09:55,752
wrongly condemned to die.

301
00:09:55,752 --> 00:09:57,551
He stepped into the sunlight,

302
00:09:57,551 --> 00:09:59,970
and he said the words that would become

303
00:09:59,970 --> 00:10:02,576
the title of his book: the sun does shine.

304
00:10:02,576 --> 00:10:04,499
He had walked into that cell at

305
00:10:04,499 --> 00:10:05,864
twenty-nine years old.

306
00:10:05,864 --> 00:10:07,663
He walked out at fifty-eight.

307
00:10:07,663 --> 00:10:09,028
Nearly three decades —

308
00:10:09,028 --> 00:10:11,199
the entire middle of a man's life —

309
00:10:11,199 --> 00:10:13,370
spent a few steps from an execution

310
00:10:13,370 --> 00:10:15,852
chamber, for a crime that was never his.

311
00:10:16,202 --> 00:10:18,344
The two murders he spent thirty years

312
00:10:18,344 --> 00:10:20,196
condemned for were never solved.

313
00:10:20,546 --> 00:10:22,810
Think about what nearly killed him.

314
00:10:22,810 --> 00:10:25,204
Not a lie anyone could see through at

315
00:10:25,204 --> 00:10:25,904
a glance —

316
00:10:25,904 --> 00:10:26,745
the opposite.

317
00:10:26,745 --> 00:10:29,462
A neat, confident, official-sounding claim

318
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:31,274
that a bullet matched a gun.

319
00:10:31,274 --> 00:10:32,762
It looked like science.

320
00:10:32,762 --> 00:10:33,927
It looked certain.

321
00:10:33,927 --> 00:10:36,191
And almost no one with the power to

322
00:10:36,191 --> 00:10:38,455
stop it ever checked whether it was

323
00:10:38,455 --> 00:10:39,308
actually true.

324
00:10:39,658 --> 00:10:42,095
That is the quiet danger in this story.

325
00:10:42,095 --> 00:10:44,720
The evidence that is most dangerous is not

326
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,533
the evidence that looks weak.

327
00:10:46,533 --> 00:10:48,970
It is the evidence that looks certain —

328
00:10:48,970 --> 00:10:51,595
because certainty is exactly what stops us

329
00:10:51,595 --> 00:10:52,845
from looking closer.

330
00:10:52,845 --> 00:10:55,407
Anthony Ray Hinton lost thirty years to a

331
00:10:55,407 --> 00:10:58,032
bullet that matched nothing, and a defense

332
00:10:58,032 --> 00:10:59,219
that could not see.

333
00:10:59,219 --> 00:11:01,032
He walked out with his humor,

334
00:11:01,032 --> 00:11:02,969
his imagination, and his life —

335
00:11:02,969 --> 00:11:05,469
which, when a state spends three decades

336
00:11:05,469 --> 00:11:07,532
trying to end it, is its own kind

337
00:11:07,532 --> 00:11:08,232
of victory.

338
00:11:08,569 --> 00:11:10,418
If a system could do this to an

339
00:11:10,418 --> 00:11:12,685
innocent man, and very nearly get away

340
00:11:12,685 --> 00:11:13,819
with it, hit like —

341
00:11:13,819 --> 00:11:16,324
so that it's a little harder to look away.
